"It is narrated that when Abu Talib fell ill, the Quraysh came and complained that the Messenger of Allah was speaking ill of their idols. Abu Talib called for his nephew. When our Prophet proposed to them that they say \"lâ ilâhe illallah\", the disbelievers jumped up in agitation and said, \"Has he made the gods into one single god? This is truly an astonishing thing!\" Thus, the first seven verses of Surah Sad were revealed concerning this event.\n\nThe letter \"Sâd\" at the beginning of the surah is among the disjointed/mutashabih letters (huruf al-muqatta'at). It has been said that this is a challenge, the name of the surah, or that it points to meanings such as truthfulness. In addition, it carries the meaning of \"sadâ\" (the reverberating echo of a sound); in this case, it may be a command to respond to the Qur'an. In some narrations, it is stated that this letter is the name of a sea beneath the Throne of Allah.\n\nThe word \"dhikr\" (remembrance/zikir) here can bear three meanings: honor and glory, counsel and reminder, and explaining the requirements of the religion. The response to this oath has been elided; however, its meaning is the truthfulness of the Prophet and the certainty of the arrival of the promised punishment. The disbelievers, on the other hand, are entrenched in pride and discord; they do not accept tawhid (the oneness of Allah). They say, \"We have not heard of this in the latest religion (millet-i ahire)\"; this is an indication that, like the latter community (the Christians), they too accepted the Trinity.\n\nThe disbelievers, jealous of the revelation of the Qur'an, objected by saying, \"Has this reminder been sent down to him from among us?\" They are a mere remnant, like the routed confederate armies of the past. These verses point to the Quraysh's defeat at Badr. In what follows, it is recounted that the disbelievers mockingly demanded the punishment be hastened. Patience is counseled to the Prophet, and he is asked to remember David (Davud), a powerful servant.\n\nDavid (peace be upon him) was a strong prophet who remembered and praised Allah abundantly. The mountains and birds would glorify Allah alongside him. Allah had granted him wisdom and the ability to distinguish truth from falsehood. One day, two litigants climbed over the wall into his prayer chamber (mihrab) and presented a dispute regarding oppression between them. David warned the wrongdoer, and realizing that he was being tested, he immediately repented and fell down in prostration. Allah forgave him and granted him nearness to Himself. Thereupon, it was proclaimed: \"O David! We have made you a viceregent upon the earth; so judge with truth, and do not follow whim and desire.\"\n\nThe heavens, the earth, and what is between them were not created in vain. The presumption of the disbelievers is the contrary. Those who believe and perform righteous deeds cannot be held equal to the corrupters. The Qur'an is a blessed book; it was sent down so that its verses might be pondered and that people of understanding might take heed.\n\nSolomon (peace be upon him) was also an excellent servant; he glorified Allah abundantly and constantly turned to Him. One late afternoon, thoroughbred horses were displayed before him. He stated that his love for horses was out of the remembrance of his Lord. Then he stroked the legs and necks of the horses. Solomon was tested; a lifeless body was placed upon his throne. He then repented and asked Allah for a dominion uniquely his own. Allah placed the wind, the devils (those who build and dive), and others under his command. This was a bounty from Allah.\n\nJob (peace be upon him) should also be remembered. At one time, he pleaded to his Lord, saying that Satan had afflicted him with distress and torment. Allah commanded him to strike the ground with his foot; from there, cool water emerged for bathing and drinking. His family was restored to him, along with the like of them in addition. Furthermore, he was told to take a bundle in his hand and strike his wife with it so as not to break his oath. Job was a patient and excellent servant; he was ever turning to Allah.\n\nThe word \"dighth\" (dığs) mentioned in this section of Surah Sad means a bundle or a handful of twigs. According to the narration, Job had sworn to strike his wife with a hundred lashes; Allah granted him the concession (rukhsah) to strike with a gathered bundle. This concession is known in Islamic jurisprudence as the \"Jobian concession\" (Eyyub ruhsatı). According to Elmalılı, this indication may also point to the necessity of establishing a community.\n\nNext, Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob are commemorated. They are described as \"possessors of hands and vision\"; that is, they were potent in righteous deeds and sound knowledge. Through a pure quality, they attained mindfulness of the Hereafter and were devoted, sincere servants. In addition, Ishmael, Elisha, and Dhul-Kifl are mentioned; all of them are among the very best. Ishmael is highlighted separately; Elisha was the successor of Elijah and was granted prophethood; and Dhul-Kifl, as the son of Job, called people to the oneness of Allah (tawhid) in the region of Damascus (Sham).\n\nThese verses serve as an admonition. For the righteous, there is a beautiful destination: the Gardens of Eden ('Adn). There, every gate is open, and diverse fruits and matchless drinks are offered. By their side are companions of equal age whose gazes are restrained solely to their spouses. This is the provision promised for the Day of Reckoning, and it will never be depleted.\n\nFor the transgressors, however, there is Hell and a wretched resting place. There, they taste boiling fluid and foul pus (ghassaq). Ghassaq, as the opposite of scalding water (hamim), is an intensely cold and foul-smelling drink. Moreover, diverse torments of similar pairs are presented to them. A dispute breaks out between the leaders and their followers; the leaders say to those who follow behind, \"No welcome for you!\" and they reply, \"Nay, it is you who have no welcome, for you brought Hell upon us!\" Then they pray against them: \"O our Lord, whoever brought this upon us, double their punishment manifold!\" Furthermore, remembering how they used to ridicule the poor believers in the world, they are astonished when they do not see them in Hell. This dispute will surely come to pass.\n\nOur Prophet (peace be upon him) is addressed thus: \"Say: I am only a warner. There is no god but Allah, the One, the All-Dominant.\" This is momentous news, yet people turn away from it. The Prophet had no knowledge of the dispute among the exalted assembly of angels (al-mala' al-a'la), but he was informed through revelation. This dispute relates to the statement mentioned in Surah al-Baqarah: \"Will You place therein one who will cause corruption?\" The secret of the dispute in the exalted assembly concerns the expiation of sins and the attainment of high ranks.\n\nAllah tells the angels that He is going to create a human being from clay. After fashioning and proportioning him, He commands them to prostrate before him once He has breathed into him of His spirit. The angels all prostrate together, but Iblis behaves arrogantly, refuses to prostrate, and becomes one of the disbelievers. Allah asks: \"What prevented you from prostrating to that which I created with My own hands?\" Iblis replies, \"I am better than him; You created me from fire, and You created him from clay.\" Thereupon, Allah banishes Iblis from Paradise and proclaims that His curse is upon him until an appointed time. Iblis asks for respite to mislead mankind, though he concedes that sincere servants will be exempt from this. Allah then declares that He will surely fill Hell with Iblis and all those who follow him.\n\nIt is said to the Prophet that he asks for no payment in return for the Qur'an and imposes nothing of his own accord. The Qur'an is an admonition for all the worlds. Everyone will surely come to know its news after a time, in this world or in the Hereafter."